            "<strong>Definition and purpose.</strong> Hygiene care is the cleansing of the skin, mouth, eyes, hair and nails of a patient who cannot do it unaided, for comfort, dignity, the prevention of infection and skin breakdown, stimulation of circulation and — not least — the single best daily opportunity a nurse has to inspect the whole skin, assess movement, pain, breathing and mood, and talk with the patient. It is delegated to the most junior person on the ward and it is where pressure injuries, oral candidiasis, early cellulitis, unreported wounds and depression are found or missed. The patient's own preferences about time, products, who washes her and how much she does herself are asked for and respected; a patient who can wash her face and hands does so, because dependence is not a treatment.",
