            "<strong>Steps and rationale.</strong> Hand hygiene; apron; identify and explain; offer the bedpan; remove the top covers and replace them with the bath blanket, removing the gown under it so that only the part being washed is exposed at any moment — exposure causes chilling and shame, and both raise the blood pressure and the pulse you will chart later. Wash in the order <strong>clean to dirty</strong>: the <strong>eyes</strong> first with water alone, inner canthus to outer with a separate part of the cloth for each eye; the <strong>face, ears and neck</strong>, soap only if the patient wishes; the <strong>far arm then the near arm</strong>, with long firm strokes from the hand towards the shoulder (distal to proximal assists venous return), the axilla, and the hands soaked in the basin and nails cleaned; the <strong>chest and abdomen</strong>, drying under the breasts and in the folds where moisture macerates skin and fungus grows; the <strong>far leg then the near leg</strong> from the ankle upwards with the knee flexed, the feet soaked in the basin, dried thoroughly between the toes; <strong>change the water</strong>; turn the patient on her side and wash the <strong>back and buttocks</strong>, then carry out the <strong>pressure-area inspection</strong> of the sacrum, hips, heels, elbows and shoulder blades and apply emollient to dry skin — a light massage of the back for comfort is acceptable, but <strong>reddened skin and bony prominences are never rubbed or massaged</strong>, because the tissue beneath is already damaged; <strong>change the water and gloves</strong>; wash the <strong>perineum last</strong>. Pat rather than rub the skin dry; apply the emollient the skin needs rather than talcum powder, which cakes in folds and abrades; replace the gown, comb the hair, clean the teeth, change the linen, position the patient on a 30-degree tilt with the heels free, lower the bed, call bell in reach; clean and dry the basin and store it inverted for that patient only; record the care, the skin inspection and anything the patient reported. The whole procedure is also a conversation: pain, appetite, sleep, worries and the family are asked about while the hands are busy.",
